MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The search page now opens with an AI entry point: describe what you
need, or ask the question the product actually exists for — "which of
these has actually turned up on the day?" — and get an answer grounded
in the seed data rather than a search form to fill in.

Retrieval (src/lib/retrieve.js) runs in the browser over the seed set.
No vector store and no embedding call: eight bands with hand-tagged
reviews is a scoring problem, not a search-infrastructure problem, and
keeping it local means retrieval cannot leak, cost anything, or fail.
It reads city, style, budget ("1 lakh", "80k", "under 90,000"), size
and reliability intent, then emits a compact fact sheet per band.

Generation is one structured Claude call over those fact sheets only,
so a band, price or review that is not in the seed set cannot appear.
The abstention rules are enforced twice — in the system prompt and
again in code:

- a flagged band is reported every time it is mentioned, never averaged
  away and never ranked out of the answer
- "Limited Info" stays insufficient rather than softening to "seems fine"
- band ids the model returns are filtered against what was retrieved
- a question matching nothing is answered without calling the model

The key never reaches the browser. A static site cannot hold a secret,
so the call lives in netlify/functions/ask.js and the client sends only
a question — never context, so a crafted request cannot feed the model
invented facts. With no endpoint set, the same retrieved facts are read
out deterministically and the UI says so rather than passing it off as
a model. Verified the built bundle contains no SDK and no key material.

Seed data and the tier assessment move to src/lib/seed.js so retrieval
reads exactly what the screens render; App.jsx keeps the tier -> colour
mapping, which is the only UI-specific part.
