From 055fd9784d12f6e7799830fd9729e5c81b587c81 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 19 Aug 2026 10:47:06 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] Ask: answer vague questions, and ask a follow-up instead
 of dead-ending
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Two problems, both of which made the feature refuse questions it could
actually answer.

"a good destination" got "nothing in the seed set matches that". It is
a real question — a destination wedding is a travel question, and the
seed set holds the outstation rate, whether there is a written contract
and whether the advance comes back. So: recognise destination/outstation
wording and lead the answer with exactly those three facts, and flag a
missing contract as the term to push on before paying an advance.

The wider bug was conflating vague with out-of-domain. "good band",
"best one", "which is worth it", "something nice" all abstained. They
are ordinary questions and the tiers already stop an answer from
becoming a fabricated recommendation, so they are now answered from
what the evidence supports, with a closing clause on what would narrow
it. Abstention is kept for what it was for: genuinely unplaceable input.

And when input really is unplaceable, it no longer dead-ends. It asks
one targeted question — city, then style, then budget — with tappable
options, folds the answer back into the query, and retrieves again.
Each answer is real signal, so one round usually reaches results; after
two the best-evidenced bands are shown regardless. Never a third
question, never a dead end.

Also fixed: a bare number anywhere in a question was read as a budget
("xyzzy 12345" became a ₹12,345 cap), so bare digits now need money
context; and a leftover ternary whose branches were identical.

The follow-up response carries no bandIds, which crashed the render to
a blank page before the clarify branch was reached — guarded.
